@flexiarg argument-coherence/one-worked-instance
@title Show One Worked Instance
@keywords pilot, example, case, implementation, small scale
@audience paper and proposal authors, reviewers, who-benefits analysts
@tone normative
@style pattern
@grade principle
@provenance Mined from the Peeragogy "Greatest Flops" archive (~/notes/peeragogy-flops): the three LAK 2011 reviews of flop 1 (appendices/01, 'Reviews'); Joe's five-question analysis of flop 1 in the outtakes (appendices/07). Drafted by claude-16 with Joe, 2026-10-29.
@status draft 2026-10-29; to test blind on the other flops, with Corneli 2016 (ICCC) and the Peeragogy Handbook as controls that should pass
@references [argument-coherence/ARGUMENT argument-coherence/evidence-the-venue-counts argument-coherence/earn-the-coined-term argument-coherence/say-how-it-carries-back argument-coherence/show-the-deliverable argument-coherence/plan-at-reviewable-grain argument-coherence/claims-as-hypotheses]

! conclusion:
  Before generalising, show the idea working, or failing informatively, in one small real case that the authors were part of.

  + context:
    Authors who ran a course or project often treat it as background and argue from principles. Reviewers want the case.

  + IF:
    The argument proposes a general approach that the authors have already tried somewhere.

  + HOWEVER:
    A single case can be unrepresentative, and a full study may be out of reach.

  + THEN:
    Describe the case you have: what was done, what happened, and which principle it bears on. Mark it as one case.

  + BECAUSE:
    Flop 1's authors had run two P2PU courses, yet the paper argued from principles. Review 1: 'Since the author has organised course based on "Paragogy", a synthesis approach on the course and the principles applied should provide better understanding.' Joe's analysis: 'A pilot study that explains how paragogy was used in a small-scale example would be helpful.'

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Test blind: a reviewer given only a flop and this family; compare its rows with the recorded verdicts.]
    next[Related: writing-coherence/floating-formalism covers unanchored abstraction in prose; this covers missing cases in the argument.]
